  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1236 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nUpper level trough currently off the southern California coast \nwill swing across west central Texas on Saturday. Large scale lift\nalong with increasing moisture levels will lead to increasing \nrain chances during the day. The primary upper level low over the \nRockies then dives down into the southern Plains on Sunday. Models\nare in a little better agreement on the track of the low. \nHowever, there remains some spread amongst the ensembles on\nrainfall totals and where the highest amounts occur. But \ncurrently the eastern half of the area is favored for the highest \namounts- potentially around an inch. Rain chances taper off by \nMonday as the upper low exits off to the east of the region and a \ndry cold front drops southward through the area. Temperatures on \nSunday will be tricky as some of the guidance keeps highs in the \n50s across the northern two-thirds of the area during the \nafternoon due to low level north-northeast flow and cold air \nadvection. Won't go quite this cold but will continue to monitor \nmodel trends. Otherwise, cool conditions will prevail on Monday- \nhighs in the 50s for most of the area- before temperatures \ngradually warm back up into the 60s through the end of the work \nweek.",
